.TH WM-DIALPAD 1
.SH NAME
wm/dialpad \- telephone keypad
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wm/dialpad
[
.B -q
] [
.B -a
.I audiodev
] [
.B -p
.I phonedir
] [
.B -s
.I speechdir
] [
.B -k
.I keysfile
] [
.I number
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Dialpad
is a phone's keypad for a touch screen: the twelve keys, each digit
with its letters, the number typed above them, Call and delete.
Call dials the number by writing
.BI "dial " number
to
.IB phonedir /phone
.RB ( /phone ,
the phone device
.BR #f ,
described in
.BR /docs/HELLAPHONE.md ),
the file an agent's
.B dial
tool writes, so what the radio does with it is the platform's: Android
places the call and shows its own call screen, iOS asks the user to
confirm it.
Where
.I phonedir
is a phone's
.B /phone
mounted over 9P, the call goes out on the phone.
A
.I number
given is typed in at the start.
.PP
The star key and the stars in the number are drawn as the sextile,
six arms 60 degrees apart with one upright, the star Bell Labs drew
for the Touch-Tone keypad, rather than a font's asterisk; what is
dialled is
.BR * ,
and the sextile's own rune (⚹, U+26B9) typed is taken for one.
.PP
A key types when the finger lifts, the key under it then, so a finger
that lands on the wrong key slides to the right one.
Held, or with button 3 (what a touch held becomes), 0 types
.BR + ,
which is taken only as a number's first character, and delete clears
the number.
Call with no number dials the last one again.
The keyboard types the same: the digits,
.BR * ,
.B #
and
.BR + ;
Backspace deletes, Escape clears, Enter calls.
.SS Touch tones
Each key sounds its Touch-Tone pair while it is held, and for at least
120 ms when it is only tapped: its row's low tone, 697, 770, 852 or
941 Hz, with its column's high one, 1209, 1336 or 1477 Hz (the fourth
column, 1633 Hz, is the A to D keys this keypad has not got).
Bell Labs chose the frequencies so that no pair is a harmonic of
another and speech seldom makes one; the high tone is a little the
louder, as a line loses more of it.
A press slid to another key changes the pair under the finger.
Taps faster than their tones queue, each sounding in turn.
The tones go to
.I audiodev
.RB ( /dev/audio ,
.IR audio (3))
as 16-bit stereo at 44.1 kHz, set through its
.B ctl
file where it has one, and are paced to the time they take, so a key
held does not fill the device's queue.
.B -q
is silence; a device that will not open is reported once and left.
.PP
In a call, a digit is not added to the number: it is sent down the
line by a write of
.BI "dtmf " digit
to the phone file, for the menus and voicemail at the other end, and
the line under the number says it was sent, or why not.
What sends it is the platform's (a modem's
.BR AT+VTS );
Android lets only the default phone app send tones into a call, and
says so.
.SS Voice
The microphone key, at the bottom left, listens: it writes
.B start
to
.IB speechdir /hear
.RB ( /mnt/speech ,
.IR speech9p ),
red while it does, and reads what was said.
That is read as
.IR dialspeech (2)
reads it: a number said as digits
.RB ( "five five five one two" ,
.BR "nine one one" ,
.BR "double two" ,
.BR "eight hundred" ,
.BR star ,
.BR pound ,
.BR plus )
or written as one
.RB ( 555-1234 ),
or a name, with the kind of number wanted
.RB ( "Sarah at work" ,
.BR "Mom's mobile" ),
found in
.IB phonedir /contactsR,
the address book the agent's
.B contacts
tool reads (a mobile number first when no kind is said; two people of
the name are named, and neither rung).
What is found is put on the keypad.
Said after
.B call
or
.BR dial ,
it is dialled three seconds later, the line under the number saying
who; a touch or a key in that time stops it, and does nothing else, so
a name heard wrong is not rung.
.PP
A number too long for its line is shown in smaller digits, and only one
too long even so loses its start, to an ellipsis: the start (a
.BR + ,
a country) is what says where it rings.
.SS Calls
Reads of the phone file are the call's state, a line
.RI \&" state " " handle " " time \&"
each.
While a call is
.BR dialing ,
.BR incoming ,
.B ringing
or
.BR connected ,
Call is a red Hang up, which writes
.BR hangup ;
.B disconnected
makes it Call again.
Where the platform reports no state, as on Android, whose call screen
has the call, Call stays Call.
The line under the number says what happened, or why a dial was
refused: no
.BR /phone ,
the radio's error, or the device's limit of ten dials a minute.
.PP
.B -k
writes the keys' places to
.I keysfile
each time they are laid out, a line
.RI \&" name " " minx " " miny " " maxx " " maxy \&"
each, as
.IR wm-osk (1)'s
.B keys
file: the digits as themselves,
.BR star ,
.BR hash ,
.BR call ,
.B delete
and
.BR mic ,
and the number shown above them,
.BR number .
.SH FILES
.TF /phone/phone
.TP
.B /phone/phone
dials, hangs up and reports the call's state
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/wm/dialpad.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR wm-osk (1),
.IR dialspeech (2)
.SH BUGS
.B answer
has no key: an incoming call is answered on the platform's own call
screen.
